artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a cross-device federated learning system in which a central server maintains a global model and trains it over a sequence of rounds. In each round, the server selects a subset of client devices (drawn from a large heterogeneous population such as mobile phones or edge devices), each holding a private local dataset, and aggregates updates from those clients that successfully complete training and report back within a round deadline.

Whether a given device contributes to a round depends on several stochastic, time-varying, and device-dependent factors, including:
- the device's availability profile (e.g., diurnal patterns, charging or idle status, Wi-Fi connectivity);
- device hardware performance (CPU/GPU speed, memory);
- network connectivity quality and bandwidth;
- current CPU load and contention with other applications;
- the size of the local dataset (which affects local computation time);
- round timeouts imposed by the server;
- other system-level constraints affecting participation.

Let $\mathcal{P}$ denote the population distribution of client data and let $\mathcal{P}_{\text{eff}}$ denote the effective distribution of data actually contributing to training under the participation process induced by the above factors. Define the *system-induced bias* of a federated learning protocol as a suitable discrepancy between the trained global model (or its loss/accuracy profile across subpopulations) and the model that would be obtained under uniform, factor-independent participation from $\mathcal{P}$.

Problem. Develop a rigorous framework that:
(a) defines and quantifies system-induced bias in cross-device federated learning as a function of the availability and system-performance factors listed above, providing measurable metrics computable from observable round-level telemetry;
(b) establishes provable bounds relating these metrics to the resulting deviation in the learned global model's behaviour on $\mathcal{P}$ versus $\mathcal{P}_{\text{eff}}$;
(c) proposes algorithmic mitigation strategies (such as biased-aware client sampling, importance reweighting, or aggregation correction) that provably reduce this bias while respecting the cross-device constraints of limited per-client communication, privacy, and unpredictable participation.

